> 
> For the two that actually are adjectives (horned and barbed), that
> does seem like the most plausible explanation. I'd instinctively
> have assumed, however, that an ice devil _lived_ around ice and was
> well adapted for dealing with it, rather than being made of it.
> (Like a freshwater salmon, a mountain goat or a sea cow. Or, come to
> think of it, a plains centaur or a hill orc.) So perhaps I would
> expect an ice devil to be unlike most demons in being cold
> resistant, but I wouldn't expect it to actually turn into water when
> attacked with fire.

I wouldn't expect that either; if not only for demons being mythical
and have their own magic. I would have thought of them as consisting
of ice, and dealing ice damage. Their body could be so cold that the
amount of energy necessary to melt it would be too huge in practice.
As with some Cthulhu demons they could usually be residents of outer
space.

Nethack actually describes them as

   ice devil
     Ice devils are large semi-insectoid creatures, who are
     equally at home in the fires of Hell and the cold of Limbo,
     and who can cause the traveller to feel the latter with just
     a touch of their tail.

And the monster properties reflect exactly that description...

     ATTK(AT_STNG, AD_COLD, 3, 4)
     MR_FIRE|MR_COLD|MR_POISON

which also fits Martin's reference.

